COLD OPEN / TEASER

FADE IN:

EXT. THE WOODS — NEIGHBORHOOD STREET — EARLY EVENING

The neighborhood settles into the hour before streetlights. Not quiet. Not loud. The specific frequency of a block that is always paying attention.

A four-family building. Low and wide. The kind of building that has been here longer than any of its current residents.

A DOG — small, tan, watchful — sits on the front step. This is KIZZY. She is facing the street.

A BOY, fourteen, comes around the corner. This is MANNY. He is not rushing. He is reading. His eyes move over the block the way someone reads a room they have been in a hundred times: not looking for anything new, but confirming that nothing old has changed.

He checks the building's front door. He checks the driveway. He checks the corner where the older boys sometimes stand. Corner is clear. He relaxes — slightly, not completely.

Kizzy gets up and moves to him.

MANNY (V.O.)
The Woods taught me something school never did. It taught me how to read a face before the face decided what it was going to be. Taught me how to cross the street before a situation became a situation. Taught me that a small thing — a wrong look, a borrowed dollar, a girl someone decided was spoken for — could follow you to your front door if you weren't paying attention.

MANNY (V.O.)
Most people call it a neighborhood. We called it the test. The test never stopped.

Manny reaches the front step. He sits next to Kizzy. She leans against him. He watches the street.

From inside the building — from the apartment low in the building, near the basement hallway — the sound of a television.

TITLE CARD: DARK SQUARES

===

ACT ONE

INT. ELLA MAE'S APARTMENT — CONTINUOUS

Small. Specific. Everything in it earned.

ELLA MAE, late thirties, moves through the kitchen with the efficiency of a person who has a shift to get to and a house to hold together before she leaves.

KEITH, ten years old, sits at the kitchen table with his homework in front of him and his eyes on something else entirely.

ELLA MAE
Keith. That homework.

KEITH
I'm doing it.

ELLA MAE
You're looking at the wall.

KEITH
I do both.

Ella Mae gives him a look. Keith looks at the homework.

ELLA MAE
Manny's home?

KEITH
He's on the steps.

She nods. She moves to the window. She can see the front step from here — just barely, at an angle. Manny and Kizzy, watching the street. Something in her face: relief she will not say out loud.

===

INT. ELLA MAE'S APARTMENT — DOORWAY — MOMENTS LATER

Ella Mae stands in the doorway in her work uniform. Work bag at her shoulder. She looks at Manny and Keith until they are both listening.

ELLA MAE
The house rule.

This is not a question.

MANNY
The house rule. Yes.

ELLA MAE
What is it?

MANNY
Whatever's out there stays out there.

ELLA MAE
Whatever's out there stays out there. And?

MANNY
And if you bring it in, it's on you.

She holds his look for a moment.

ELLA MAE
Don't be out late. Streetlights come on, you're in.

MANNY
I know.

ELLA MAE
I know you know. Say it anyway.

MANNY
Streetlights come on, we're in.

She pulls the door behind her. The lock clicks.

MANNY (V.O.)
She did not say it like a suggestion. She said it like scripture.

Manny sits with Kizzy. The street settles into the hour.

MANNY (V.O.)
Whatever happened outside stayed outside. Whatever pride, disrespect, misunderstanding, or trouble followed a person home had to die before it reached her living room.

Keith appears at the doorway, watching his brother.

===

EXT. THE WOODS — LATER

Manny and Keith ride bikes through the block. Kizzy races behind them.

They pass a stickball game. A basketball bouncing on cracked pavement. Older boys carrying a boom box.

MANNY (V.O.)
Manny understood that rule better than anyone. He was the oldest. Keith was his little brother. Four years separated them, but in the Woods, four years could feel like a whole lifetime.

Keith swerves close to Manny, grinning. Manny reaches out and steadies him before he can clip the curb.

MANNY (V.O.)
Manny was not Keith's father. But he carried pieces of that job anyway.

===

EXT. EMPTY LOT / ALLEY — LATE AFTERNOON

Older kids call Keith over. Keith heads toward them without thinking. Manny catches the back of his shirt.

MANNY
Not over there.

KEITH
I'm just talking.

MANNY
Talk from here.

Keith rolls his eyes, but stays. Kizzy plants herself beside Manny's leg.

MANNY (V.O.)
Some boys attract friends. Some attract trouble. Keith had a way of attracting both.

===

ACT TWO

EXT. THE WOODS — NIGHT

The streetlights are on.

KEITH
You know Steve?

MANNY
Which Steve.

KEITH
The one with all the records. He's got turntables in his room. James said he lets people come listen.

Manny files this away. Does not respond immediately.

KEITH
James said it sounds like a whole radio station.

Manny looks toward the older boys. Then back at his brother.

MANNY
You doing your homework?

KEITH
Why you always change the subject?

MANNY
Because when I ask about homework and you don't answer, that tells me something.

Keith makes a face. Kicks a stone down the alley. Kizzy chases it.

KEITH
Homework's done.

MANNY
Then we can go in.

===

EXT. THE WOODS — CONTINUOUS

The brothers ride side by side beneath the streetlights.

MANNY (V.O.)
In those days, the Woods felt bigger than the whole world. They had no idea they would one day move away from it.

Keith laughs at something only he understands. Manny watches him, protective and quiet.

They stop at the building's front entrance.

KEITH
You ever scared?

MANNY
Of what.

KEITH
I don't know. Everything.

MANNY
Yeah. Sometimes.

KEITH
What do you do?

MANNY
Keep moving. Read the room. Make sure you're with me.

KEITH
That's it?

MANNY
That's a lot.

Keith nods. Kizzy noses at the building's front door.

===

ACT THREE

EXT. THE WOODS — THE FOLLOWING DAYS

MONTAGE — The brothers' ordinary life in the Woods:

Manny helps Keith with math at the kitchen table. Keith draws pictures in the margins when he thinks Manny isn't looking. Manny sees. Says nothing.

The brothers play stickball with neighborhood kids. Manny lets Keith win a close call. Keith does not notice.

Manny walks the long way around the corner where the older boys gather when he and Keith are coming home. Keith clocks the route without asking why.

Kizzy curls at the foot of the brothers' shared room. Outside, the block is not quiet.

Early morning. Ella Mae leaves for the early shift. She stops at the boys' doorway. Watches them sleep. Pulls the door closed.

MANNY (V.O.)
The Woods was not just a neighborhood. It was a classroom, a warning, a playground, and a test. It taught boys how to run, how to hide, how to laugh when nothing was funny, and how to read a face before a word came out of a mouth.

===

EXT. CORNER NEAR JUNKYARD — DAY

Manny and Keith stand at the edge of the junkyard, looking in.

KEITH
This is our kingdom.

MANNY
Yeah.

KEITH
Future emperors of the junkyard.

MANNY
You read that in a book or you made that up?

KEITH
Both.

Manny laughs. Kizzy barks at a pigeon that wanders too close.

MANNY (V.O.)
That was Keith. He could build an empire in his head so fast it felt real by the time he finished the sentence.

===

INT. ELLA MAE'S APARTMENT — EVENING

Ella Mae at the table with bills and a cup of coffee going cold. Manny sits nearby, doing homework.

ELLA MAE
You keeping your brother close?

MANNY
Yes, ma'am.

ELLA MAE
Not too close. He needs room.

MANNY
I know.

ELLA MAE
But close enough.

MANNY
I know.

She nods. Looks back at the bills. Then back at him.

ELLA MAE
You hear about the Reyes boy?

MANNY
I heard.

ELLA MAE
Then you know why the rule exists.

She picks up the coffee. It's cold. She does not put it back down.

ELLA MAE
This house is the one thing the street doesn't get. You understand me?

MANNY
Yes, ma'am.

ELLA MAE
Good. Go to sleep.

Manny closes his book. But he does not go to sleep yet. He sits with it.

===

ACT FOUR

EXT. THE WOODS — GRAVEYARD — NIGHT

Night. The graveyard at the edge of the neighborhood. Iron fence and the specific dark of a place that is never fully lit.

Manny and Keith stand at the gate. Keith insisted. Manny came because Keith came.

KEITH
You go first.

MANNY
No.

KEITH
You're older.

MANNY
That means I know better.

KEITH
(already moving)
I'll go first.

He pushes open the iron gate. Manny catches it before it closes behind him. They walk in together.

The graveyard is just a graveyard. Headstones. Grass worn thin. Quiet.

Keith stands in the middle of it and holds his arms out.

KEITH
I'm not scared.

MANNY
Okay.

KEITH
I'm really not scared.

MANNY
I believe you.

KEITH
Are you scared?

Manny looks around. The dark. The stones. The gate still slightly open behind them.

MANNY
Yeah.

KEITH
Yeah?

MANNY
A little.

Keith thinks about this. Then decides it is the most honest and correct answer he has ever received.

KEITH
Cool.

They stand in the graveyard for another moment. Kizzy waits at the gate. She will not come in.

===

EXT. THE WOODS — CONTINUOUS — WALKING HOME

The brothers walk home through the empty streets.

MANNY (V.O.)
The graveyard bet was their thing. It always would be. Years later, when the world had gotten complicated in ways they could not have imagined from that night, they would still know: they had stood in the dark together. They had not run.

Keith walks close. Close enough that their shoulders occasionally touch.

MANNY (V.O.)
Before the Rocks, before Lucky's studio, before Sarah, before the party that would carry a name nobody wanted to say out loud — there was the Woods.

Kizzy moves at their heels.

MANNY (V.O.)
And the Woods, in its own way, loved them back.

===

TAG

EXT. THE WOODS — NIGHT

The brothers ride bikes side by side beneath the streetlights one last time.

Their building waits. Kizzy runs between their wheels.

MANNY (V.O.)
This is what childhood sounds like from the inside of it. Before you know it is childhood. Before you understand what it costs to leave it behind.

The building's front door opens. The lobby light spills out.

The boys disappear into it.

Kizzy follows.

The door closes.

MANNY (V.O.)
Some tests you pass. Some tests change what passing means.

The Woods was still out there. It always would be.

FADE OUT.

END OF EPISODE

